    {
      "id": "claim_72b728f8e4904b1ea0027a8d51f4f2d0",
      "statement": "Engine._record_derive maintains the parent/child DAG edge sets and appends the derive trace event in one place, so the graph structure and the audit trail can never diverge.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://engine.py#L83-L87",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b9f6a2574d5d028fd919c094e94486f939701d375ab0a5459edc62736a633172:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_14d6df69fdf44414bd2ad1c1a73c49fa",
      "statement": "deduce() is the backward-chaining entry point: it registers givens, resets the per-run memo, resolves the target's fact_kind via _derive_kind with default max_depth=10, returns a RESOLVED result with the derived value plus a synthetic TARGET-REACHED derive event on success, and returns an UNKNOWN result with an ev_unknown event (reason 'no rule could derive the target from available givens' plus an evidence hint) when no rule can derive it.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://engine.py#L90-L104",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2e0b48b1542aba51fcc2d6277e8c14af2cfac9525d16d4678e882ac4017a6a8a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5a6fb247db1f446e998c0889b2506515",
      "statement": "Deduction guards terminate and prevent circularity: a kind already in the visiting set is rejected with an ev_reject 'circular' event, depth greater than max_depth records an ev_deadend 'max depth exceeded' (depth increments per recursion level, so max_depth=10 bounds the longest goal chain), and both success and failure are memoized per kind in _memo so a kind is resolved at most once per run; the memo is reset at the start of each deduce run.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://engine.py#L106-L120",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4e78e51fbea37a88fbbc36c8bac6d80df3743152d24800311d1ed4c92cd47a9d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4bcbee954b6d4eada9988cd89b773898",
      "statement": "Deduction short-circuits on known facts: any fact of the goal kind already in the store with a value and status truth, unverified_truth, or derived satisfies the sub-goal; when several candidates exist an ev_join event names them all and the first is memoized; the target fact itself never matches because it carries status target and value None.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://engine.py#L122-L130",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c092ec94f70efacea7eeee3020db42947d9c5ce5f1ba6bda3480ec53042df0f8:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_59d5a493b1944545b9394521eac91307",
      "statement": "Per-kind rule search in deduction resolves each rule input kind as a recursive sub-goal with the parent kind added to the visiting set; an unresolvable input records an ev_deadend 'needed for rule', a failing precondition or relation exception records an ev_reject with the reason, the ancestor guard rejects a derived output that is an ancestor of any input, and only then is the fact added, the derive recorded, and the kind memoized — with all rules exhausted memoized as failure via 'no rule or known fact'.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://engine.py#L132-L174",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:17e1177e75d4499ed497a93def94f4fc78b7fb7ed2b8d265000e79fb617163d0:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c366c46bcbeb4e418b7866adfcd8a9d0",
      "statement": "The ancestor closure used by both loop guards is computed transitively from the _parents edge set via _ancestors.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://engine.py#L176-L185",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:37c8a250e28aac28dd49b8c0120806980bfe386611ae9632225c34f2e26eae5b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_51f776d227124e5e9f322945e87f3af0",
      "statement": "induce() implements forward fixed-point closure per ARCH §2.1: rounds bounded by default max_rounds=8, every rule tried each round, input subsets shaped by _combos, an applied-set keyed by (rule name, sorted input ids) so a firing is never retried, precondition rejections recorded as ev_reject, an ancestor guard that skips outputs which are ancestors of inputs, dedup via add_fact with an ev_join event when a rule re-derives a known fact, an early RESOLVED return when the derived id equals target_id, and an early break when a round produces nothing new — otherwise an ev_unknown 'no rule produced the target' event and an UNKNOWN result.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://engine.py#L188-L231",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:22655fd4d5d6e0abd7fc4e72b28f9cd0fe0830cf74d1190b56e9c4ddbc67fd93:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a3006ce20736468a94a88ffb05aa42ce",
      "statement": "_combos yields the cartesian product of frontier facts grouped by fact_kind, shaped like rule.input_kinds — exactly one fact per kind, in order — and yields nothing when any required kind is missing from the frontier.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://engine.py#L233-L249",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:92836743236cbcae07457a4b46513290082030a2427c6d029b80ba93c8c53f66:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_08ba6f53e47940e59679f5d87b1132b7",
      "statement": "The derivation DAG is enforced by construction through two invariants — deduplication on fact identity and a rule forbidding derivation of a fact that is an ancestor of any of its inputs — and loops are disallowed as an explicit correctness/explainability decision, not a performance shortcut: a cycle would let a rule prove a fact using a fact it itself depends on, breaking the strictly decreasing walk back to truth axioms, turning the trace into confusion, and disallowing loops also guarantees forward search terminates [v1].",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://ARCHITECTURE.md#L120-L140",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7647260d22486066c927cefa6e88c56bf7c73118e7ac1e04d1ad838f3ed509b7:"
